1
00:00:02,116 --> 00:00:03,959
Let's start with some theory though.

2
00:00:04,745 --> 00:00:07,390
Deno is browser compatible,

3
00:00:08,112 --> 00:00:10,693
and I put that between quotes because it

4
00:00:10,693 --> 00:00:13,418
does not mean that you can take any Deno

5
00:00:13,498 --> 00:00:15,903
program and run the same code in the

6
00:00:15,903 --> 00:00:16,384
browser.

7
00:00:17,090 --> 00:00:19,318
For one, this does not work because

8
00:00:20,312 --> 00:00:22,492
Deno is able to use TypeScript, the browser

9
00:00:22,492 --> 00:00:25,442
is not. And in addition, Deno of course

10
00:00:25,458 --> 00:00:28,536
exposes certain features like working with

11
00:00:28,569 --> 00:00:29,498
the file system,

12
00:00:30,156 --> 00:00:31,855
which simply are not supported by the

13
00:00:31,855 --> 00:00:32,320
browser.

14
00:00:32,881 --> 00:00:35,798
But what it means is that where it makes

15
00:00:35,862 --> 00:00:36,455
sense,

16
00:00:37,209 --> 00:00:40,639
APIs that you find in the browser are also

17
00:00:40,671 --> 00:00:42,306
made available in Deno.

18
00:00:42,899 --> 00:00:43,749
For example,

19
00:00:44,438 --> 00:00:47,211
setting a timer with setTimeout, or sending

20
00:00:47,324 --> 00:00:50,626
an HTTP request with fetch, or listening to

21
00:00:50,626 --> 00:00:53,639
the startup of the script basically with the

22
00:00:53,720 --> 00:00:54,794
onload event.

23
00:00:55,643 --> 00:00:56,493
These are all

24
00:00:57,487 --> 00:00:58,676
all APIs

25
00:00:59,270 --> 00:01:02,256
that are available in the browser. You could

26
00:01:02,256 --> 00:01:04,889
set a timer with setTimeout in the browser.

27
00:01:05,274 --> 00:01:08,389
You can send an HTTP request with fetch in

28
00:01:08,485 --> 00:01:10,749
the browser. So this all works in the

29
00:01:10,813 --> 00:01:13,896
browser and this all also works in Deno

30
00:01:13,896 --> 00:01:14,426
scripts.

31
00:01:15,325 --> 00:01:17,974
And that's important. They are available on

32
00:01:17,974 --> 00:01:20,559
the global window object in Deno.

33
00:01:21,282 --> 00:01:23,995
So even though, if you think about it, we

34
00:01:23,995 --> 00:01:27,044
don't really have a window for our demo

35
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:29,657
script. It's not running in the browser

36
00:01:29,689 --> 00:01:30,426
window at least.

37
00:01:31,068 --> 00:01:33,953
Still, there is a global window object where

38
00:01:34,098 --> 00:01:37,128
these core functionalities are exposed. And

39
00:01:37,128 --> 00:01:39,853
again, the idea is to be as close to the

40
00:01:39,853 --> 00:01:41,296
browser as possible

41
00:01:42,098 --> 00:01:43,236
because that's another

42
00:01:43,957 --> 00:01:45,673
probably minor flaw

43
00:01:46,202 --> 00:01:50,274
that the creator of Node found in Node after

44
00:01:50,274 --> 00:01:51,540
it was created, obviously,

45
00:01:52,214 --> 00:01:55,500
that in Node, certain things are not

46
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:58,147
available that are available in the browser

47
00:01:58,452 --> 00:02:00,747
and that would make sense in Node as well,

48
00:02:01,437 --> 00:02:04,197
or if they are available, some things are

49
00:02:04,309 --> 00:02:07,021
named differently. For example, there is no

50
00:02:07,278 --> 00:02:09,428
window object in Node.

51
00:02:10,327 --> 00:02:13,553
And he wanted to fix this for Deno so that

52
00:02:13,553 --> 00:02:16,409
we basically don't have to learn a lot of

53
00:02:16,457 --> 00:02:19,362
new things, or at least not as much, but

54
00:02:19,410 --> 00:02:22,282
that we can reuse our knowledge which we

55
00:02:22,363 --> 00:02:24,545
already have from the browser. And if we

56
00:02:24,545 --> 00:02:27,353
wanna send an HTTP request, which you

57
00:02:27,353 --> 00:02:30,243
sometimes wanna do in a server-side code

58
00:02:30,243 --> 00:02:32,651
snippet as well, for example, you can just

59
00:02:32,651 --> 00:02:34,737
use the Fetch API and you don't have to

60
00:02:34,737 --> 00:02:37,402
learn about a new Deno-exclusive API for

61
00:02:37,450 --> 00:02:37,900
doing that.

62
00:02:38,429 --> 00:02:41,206
That's the idea of making it browser

63
00:02:41,399 --> 00:02:43,004
compatible between quotes.

64
00:02:43,951 --> 00:02:46,102
Now let's take a closer look at that and in

65
00:02:46,199 --> 00:02:46,584
general,

66
00:02:47,162 --> 00:02:49,714
let's take a closer look at the built-in

67
00:02:49,810 --> 00:02:51,496
features Deno ships with.
